# The function field is generated over F(x) by y

Theorem statement: `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.adjoin_yCoord_eq_top`
(`79d01350-dee8-5e1f-8179-7f315d731441`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `987c4a87-b02f-5d37-828f-3c45b69ae14c`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $F$ be a field and let $W$ be an affine Weierstrass curve over $F$, with coordinate ring
$W.\mathrm{CoordinateRing}$ and function field $W.\mathrm{FunctionField}$, the latter carrying its
algebra structure over the rational function field $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,F$ (the $x$-coordinate
subfield) as set up in the definition module. Write $\mathrm{yCoord}\,W$ for the element of
$W.\mathrm{FunctionField}$ obtained by taking the class of the variable $Y$ in the coordinate ring
and mapping it along the structure map $W.\mathrm{CoordinateRing} \to W.\mathrm{FunctionField}$. The
assertion, with no further hypotheses on $F$ or on $W$, is that the intermediate field of
$W.\mathrm{FunctionField}/\mathrm{RatFunc}\,F$ generated by the single element $\mathrm{yCoord}\,W$
is the top intermediate field, i.e. equals all of $W.\mathrm{FunctionField}$: in classical notation,
$F(W) = F(x)(y)$, so that $F(W)$ is a simple extension of $F(x)$ generated by the $y$-coordinate
function.

This is the standard fact that the function field of a plane Weierstrass curve is generated over the
field of rational functions in $x$ by the coordinate function $y$. Combined with the integrality of
$\mathrm{yCoord}\,W$ over $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,F$, it yields the finite-dimensionality of
$W.\mathrm{FunctionField}$ over $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,F$ (of degree at most $2$), and it is used
further in the construction of principal divisors on the function field and in the descent to a
countable intermediate field.

